{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "robert-2026-09-09",
  "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T13:03:39.891353Z",
  "title": "The small balance that deserves more attention",
  "portfolioAsOf": "2026-09-08T21:36:38.967Z",
  "paragraphs": [
    "I want an AI that notices the fragile loan before it finds the next yield opportunity. That is the standard I care about for ARC: can better access to information change what deserves my attention? Making a portfolio easier to operate is useful. Making its weakest point harder to overlook would be more valuable.",
    "Aave has announced an official MCP server that lets AI assistants read live lending data and prepare transactions across V3 and V4. Its transaction preparation tools return unsigned requests, with signing left to the user's wallet. I like that separation of responsibilities. A position query can surface debt and health, and an action preview can show the resulting health factor before a decision becomes a transaction. Those are practical building blocks for asking better questions.",
    "My active wallet gives that idea a concrete test. Its Fluid lending position shows roughly $1,200 supplied in syrupUSDC and USDC against about $1,000 of USDC debt, with a health rate of 1.08. That number deserves attention even though the net position is only about $200. I think a system that ranks urgency by net dollar value could miss exactly this kind of exposure. The debt and the collateral behind the small headline balance matter.",
    "The Aave positions tell a different story. Both hold WETH against USDC borrowing, and their displayed health rates have edged up from 3.08 to 3.10 in the active wallet and from 2.05 to 2.07 in the cold wallet. That is a modest improvement in the displayed cushion. It does not make the Fluid position less demanding, and Aave's new connection does not itself cover that separate protocol.",
    "I would judge an assistant by whether it keeps these distinctions visible when everything else looks comfortable. Can it explain which debt needs attention, what a proposed response would change, and who still has to act? I would become more confident in agent-assisted treasury management if it consistently brought the uncomfortable position to the front, with enough context to make a deliberate decision. Faster access alone would not change my mind."
  ],
